# 🤖 Agentic CI/CD: Kubernetes Deployment Gate with Elastic MCP

> **An AI-assisted CI/CD deployment gate that checks live Kubernetes cluster health before allowing a deployment to proceed.**

[![Kubernetes](https://img.shields.io/badge/Kubernetes-Kind-326CE5?logo=kubernetes\&logoColor=white)](https://kind.sigs.k8s.io/)
[![GitHub Actions](https://img.shields.io/badge/CI-GitHub%20Actions-2088FF?logo=githubactions\&logoColor=white)](https://github.com/features/actions)
[![Argo CD](https://img.shields.io/badge/GitOps-Argo%20CD-EF7B4D?logo=argo\&logoColor=white)](https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/)
[![OpenTelemetry](https://img.shields.io/badge/Observability-OpenTelemetry-000000?logo=opentelemetry\&logoColor=white)](https://opentelemetry.io/)
[![Elastic](https://img.shields.io/badge/Observability-Elastic-005571?logo=elastic\&logoColor=white)](https://www.elastic.co/)
[![MCP](https://img.shields.io/badge/Protocol-MCP-7C3AED)](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/)

---

## 📌 Overview

Traditional CI/CD pipelines are typically **blind to the runtime state of the infrastructure**.

They validate things such as:

* ✅ Code builds successfully
* ✅ Tests pass
* ✅ Docker image is created
* ✅ Image is pushed successfully

Then they deploy.

But what if the Kubernetes cluster is already under heavy resource pressure?

This project introduces an **AI-powered Kubernetes deployment gate** that asks:

> **"Is the cluster healthy enough to safely accept a new deployment?"**

Before deployment, the pipeline queries **live Kubernetes metrics**, evaluates cluster health through an **Elastic Agent Builder Tool**, and blocks the deployment if the cluster exceeds a configured resource threshold.

The health-check capability is exposed through the **Model Context Protocol (MCP)** and called directly from **GitHub Actions**.

---

# 🎯 What This Project Demonstrates

This project combines several modern infrastructure patterns into a complete working system:

* 🤖 **Agentic infrastructure tooling**
* 🔌 **Model Context Protocol (MCP)**
* ☸️ **Kubernetes**
* 🔄 **GitOps with Argo CD**
* 📊 **OpenTelemetry observability**
* 🔎 **Elastic ES|QL**
* ⚙️ **GitHub Actions**
* 🐳 **Docker**
* 🚦 **Runtime-aware CI/CD deployment gates**

The result is a pipeline that does not simply ask:

> "Did the application build successfully?"

It also asks:

> "Is the target Kubernetes environment healthy enough to deploy?"

---

