# 🧠 Core Concept

## Traditional CI/CD

A typical pipeline looks like this:

```text
Code
 │
 ▼
Build
 │
 ▼
Test
 │
 ▼
Push Image
 │
 ▼
Deploy
```

The pipeline assumes the infrastructure is ready.

---

## Agentic CI/CD

This project introduces an additional decision point:

```text
Code
 │
 ▼
Build
 │
 ▼
Test
 │
 ▼
Push Image
 │
 ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│ Runtime Health Gate │
│                     │
│ Is the Kubernetes   │
│ cluster healthy?    │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           │
     ┌─────┴─────┐
     │           │
   Healthy     Unhealthy
     │           │
     ▼           ▼
   Deploy      Block
```

This creates a **runtime-aware deployment pipeline**.

---

# 🔍 How the Health Gate Works

The deployment gate follows the process below.

---

## 1️⃣ Kubernetes Metrics Collection

An OpenTelemetry Collector runs as a **DaemonSet** inside the Kind cluster.

The collector uses the `kubeletstats` receiver to collect Kubernetes node metrics.

Metrics include:

```text
k8s.node.cpu.usage

k8s.node.memory.usage

k8s.node.memory.available

k8s.node.memory.working_set
```

Metrics are collected every:

```text
20 seconds
```

---

## 2️⃣ Metrics Are Exported to Elastic

The OpenTelemetry Collector exports metrics using OTLP.

```text
Kind Cluster

      │

      ▼

OpenTelemetry Collector

      │
      │ OTLP
      ▼

Elastic Cloud

      │

      ▼

Elasticsearch
```

The Kubernetes resource metrics are now queryable using **ES|QL**.

---

## 3️⃣ ES|QL Calculates Cluster Memory Usage

The health check evaluates node memory usage.

The calculation is:

```text
memory_usage_pct

=

working_set

/

(working_set + available)

×

100
```

Conceptually:

```text
Node Memory

┌───────────────────────────┐
│                           │
│       Working Set         │
│                           │
├───────────────────────────┤
│                           │
│        Available          │
│                           │
└───────────────────────────┘
```

The resulting percentage is used by the deployment gate.

---

# 🧰 Elastic Agent Builder Tool

The ES|QL health-check query is registered in Elastic Agent Builder as a reusable Tool.

```text
Tool Name

k8s_node_health_check
```

The tool:

1. Queries live Kubernetes metrics.
2. Aggregates node resource information.
3. Calculates memory utilization.
4. Returns structured health data.

The Tool can be independently tested inside Kibana.

---

# 🤖 Agent Layer

An Elastic Agent Builder Agent wraps the Tool.

```text
Agent

kubernetes_analysis_agent
```

The agent provides a conversational interface for Kubernetes analysis.

For example:

```text
Is my Kubernetes cluster healthy?
```

The agent can invoke:

```text
k8s_node_health_check
```

and analyze the returned metrics.

---

# ⚠️ Important Design Decision

Although the project uses an AI Agent, the **CI/CD pipeline does not call the conversational agent directly**.

Instead:

```text
GitHub Actions

        │

        ▼

MCP

        │

        ▼

k8s_node_health_check
```

The pipeline calls the underlying Tool directly.

### Why?

AI-generated conversational responses can contain:

* Natural language
* Explanations
* Variable formatting
* Non-deterministic responses

A deployment gate should be deterministic.

Therefore:

```text
Agent
  │
  └── Used for conversational analysis

Tool
  │
  └── Used for deterministic CI/CD automation
```

This separation is intentional.

> **Use AI for analysis and reasoning. Use structured tools for deterministic automation.**

---

# 🔌 Model Context Protocol (MCP)

Elastic exposes Agent Builder tools through an MCP server.

GitHub Actions communicates with the MCP endpoint.

```text
GitHub Actions

       │

       │ MCP tools/call

       ▼

Elastic MCP Server

       │

       ▼

Agent Builder Tool

       │

       ▼

ES|QL Query

       │

       ▼

Elasticsearch Metrics
```

The pipeline receives structured JSON.

Example flow:

```text
POST

tools/call
```

↓

```json
{
  "result": {
    "content": [
      {
        "text": "{...health data...}"
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

---

# 🚦 Deployment Decision

The pipeline uses a memory threshold.

```text
Threshold

80%
```

Decision logic:

```text
Memory Usage

< 80%

      │

      ▼

✅ HEALTHY

      │

      ▼

Deployment Allowed
```

---

```text
Memory Usage

≥ 80%

      │

      ▼

❌ UNHEALTHY

      │

      ▼

Pipeline Blocked
```

The workflow exits with:

```bash
exit 1
```

This causes the health-check job to fail.

Because the deployment job depends on the health gate:

```text
deploy

needs:

k8s-health-gate
```

the deployment never executes.

---

