# 🔄 GitHub Actions Pipeline

The workflow contains three major jobs.

---

## 🐳 Job 1: Build and Push

```text
build-and-push
```

Runs on:

```text
GitHub-hosted Runner
```

Responsibilities:

* Build Docker image
* Tag image using Git commit SHA
* Authenticate with Docker Hub
* Push image

Example tag:

```text
username/agentic-cicd-app:<commit-sha>
```

---

## 🚦 Job 2: Kubernetes Health Gate

```text
k8s-health-gate
```

Runs on:

```text
Self-hosted Runner
```

Responsibilities:

```text
Call Elastic MCP

↓

Call k8s_node_health_check

↓

Receive cluster metrics

↓

Parse JSON

↓

Evaluate memory usage

↓

Compare with threshold
```

Threshold:

```text
80%
```

---

## 🚀 Job 3: Deploy

```text
deploy
```

Runs only when:

```text
k8s-health-gate

=

SUCCESS
```

Responsibilities:

```text
Update Docker image tag

↓

Update k8s/deployment.yaml

↓

Git Commit

↓

Git Push

↓

Argo CD detects change

↓

Argo CD syncs cluster
```

---

# 🛑 Deployment Blocking Behavior

The workflow contains a hard dependency.

Conceptually:

```yaml
deploy:
  needs:
    - k8s-health-gate
```

If the health check fails:

```text
k8s-health-gate

❌ FAILED
```

Then:

```text
deploy

⏭️ SKIPPED
```

The deployment is never executed.

This creates a real deployment gate.

---

# 🧪 Testing the Health Gate

The project was tested in both scenarios.

---

## ✅ Healthy Cluster

Example:

```text
Memory Usage:

45%
```

Threshold:

```text
80%
```

Result:

```text
45 < 80

↓

HEALTHY

↓

DEPLOYMENT ALLOWED
```

Pipeline:

```text
Build

↓

Push

↓

Health Gate ✅

↓

Deploy

↓

Argo CD Sync
```

---

## ❌ Unhealthy Cluster

For testing, a deliberately low threshold can be configured.

Example:

```text
Threshold:

10%
```

Cluster usage:

```text
45%
```

Result:

```text
45 > 10

↓

UNHEALTHY

↓

exit 1
```

Pipeline:

```text
Build

↓

Push

↓

Health Gate ❌

↓

PIPELINE STOPS
```

The `deploy` job does not run.

---

# ⚠️ Key Issues and Lessons Learned

## 1. MCP Requires an Accept Header

Without:

```text
Accept:
application/json, text/event-stream
```

Elastic MCP returns:

```text
406 Not Acceptable
```

---

## 2. MCP Responses Are Double-Encoded

The value:

```text
result.content[0].text
```

contains a JSON string.

Therefore:

```text
JSON

↓

String

↓

JSON
```

It must be parsed twice.

---

## 3. No `k8s.node.cpu.utilization`

The OpenTelemetry metrics do not provide:

```text
k8s.node.cpu.utilization
```

Instead:

```text
k8s.node.cpu.usage
```

represents CPU usage in fractional cores.

Therefore, CPU percentage cannot be directly evaluated without additional capacity information.

This project uses:

```text
Memory Usage %
```

as the deterministic deployment threshold.

CPU usage is included as informational context.

---

## 4. GitHub-Hosted Runners Cannot Reach Local Kubernetes

Architecture:

```text
GitHub Cloud

        ❌

        │

        │ Cannot reach

        ▼

Local Machine

        │

        ▼

Kind Cluster
```

Solution:

```text
Self-hosted Runner

        │

        ▼

Local Kind Cluster
```

---

## 5. Runner Dependencies Matter

The health gate uses:

```text
curl

jq

bc
```

Ensure these dependencies exist on the self-hosted runner.

---

